      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kearny%2C_New_Jersey",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "In 1875, the Clark Thread Company from Scotland built two large mills in Kearny, later adding two more in 1890, attracting skilled flax spinners from the British Isles.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        18,
        19,
        20,
        21
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kearny%2C_New_Jersey",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Kearny’s railroad heritage includes the abandoned Arlington station on the Erie Railroad’s Newark Branch, once a key stop for freight and passengers.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1,
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kearny%2C_New_Jersey",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The Passaic River’s former NX Bridge, now abandoned, was famously used as a set for the film “Annie.”",
